window.docContents[86].resolve({"/rules/PL1006/#PL1006-established":{"contents":"No authored unsafe or partial declaration is on the claimed surface; every admitted generated helper satisfied all §8.4 conditions.\n\nIt does not establish:\n\n* That unsafe or partial code elsewhere is logically unsound; the rule concerns evidence, not a claim that such code is wrong.* Termination proofs' adequacy for cost claims.\n\nReview obligations the rule-coverage map associates with this rule (identifiers of the checker's Residual account):\n\n* R-QUALIFY: the checker's detection is qualified for this invocation, toolchain and capability* R-COST: cost claims name their domain and rest on an argument or bounded observation* R-INTENT: the proposition expresses the intended requirement, with its quantifiers, hypotheses and limits\n\nEvery accepted result lists all residual obligations as open, whatever rules it checked: R-INTENT, R-INVARIANT, R-LAWS, R-BOUNDARY, R-NONVACUITY, R-DOC, R-COST, R-QUALIFY (and R-GRAPH for a serialized-graph claim). A listed identifier is an open obligation, never a completed review.\n\nEvery accepted result also relies on these trusted mechanisms (the checker's Trusted account), which no rule verifies:\n\n* toolchain: Lean elaborator, kernel and compiler at the snapshot's toolchain identity* acquisition: manifest parsing, Lake loading, and source, configuration and dependency acquisition* extraction: environment extraction, compiler and worker processes, and JSON transport* runtime: native runtime and every execution boundary reported as trusted\n\n","context":"Plumb for Lean rule reference\u0009Rule index\u0009PL1006: Unsafe and partial declarations require exact helper authentication","header":"What a passing result establishes","id":"/rules/PL1006/#PL1006-established"},"/rules/PL2005/#PL2005-established":{"contents":"Every owned logical declaration and its owned dependencies passed kernel replay, and the frozen sources were unchanged during the audit. A failed admission or changed source is incomplete and never accepted.\n\nIt does not establish:\n\n* Imported, unowned dependencies are not replayed; they remain the declared trusted base.* Incremental admission does not establish fresh source elaboration.\n\nReview obligations the rule-coverage map associates with this rule (identifiers of the checker's Residual account):\n\n* R-QUALIFY: the checker's detection is qualified for this invocation, toolchain and capability\n\nEvery accepted result lists all residual obligations as open, whatever rules it checked: R-INTENT, R-INVARIANT, R-LAWS, R-BOUNDARY, R-NONVACUITY, R-DOC, R-COST, R-QUALIFY (and R-GRAPH for a serialized-graph claim). A listed identifier is an open obligation, never a completed review.\n\nEvery accepted result also relies on these trusted mechanisms (the checker's Trusted account), which no rule verifies:\n\n* toolchain: Lean elaborator, kernel and compiler at the snapshot's toolchain identity* acquisition: manifest parsing, Lake loading, and source, configuration and dependency acquisition* extraction: environment extraction, compiler and worker processes, and JSON transport* runtime: native runtime and every execution boundary reported as trusted\n\n","context":"Plumb for Lean rule reference\u0009Rule index\u0009PL2005: Required admission and source evidence must be complete","header":"What a passing result establishes","id":"/rules/PL2005/#PL2005-established"},"/rules/PL4003/#PL4003-established":{"contents":"The negative fence completed with a source rejection whose single error message matches the whole pattern.\n\nIt does not establish:\n\n* That the rejection happens for the conceptual reason the prose explains beyond the matched message text.\n\nReview obligations the rule-coverage map associates with this rule (identifiers of the checker's Residual account):\n\n* R-QUALIFY: the checker's detection is qualified for this invocation, toolchain and capability* R-DOC: documentation is complete and faithful to the declarations and requirements\n\nEvery accepted result lists all residual obligations as open, whatever rules it checked: R-INTENT, R-INVARIANT, R-LAWS, R-BOUNDARY, R-NONVACUITY, R-DOC, R-COST, R-QUALIFY (and R-GRAPH for a serialized-graph claim). A listed identifier is an open obligation, never a completed review.\n\nEvery accepted result also relies on these trusted mechanisms (the checker's Trusted account), which no rule verifies:\n\n* toolchain: Lean elaborator, kernel and compiler at the snapshot's toolchain identity* acquisition: manifest parsing, Lake loading, and source, configuration and dependency acquisition* extraction: environment extraction, compiler and worker processes, and JSON transport* runtime: native runtime and every execution boundary reported as trusted\n\n","context":"Plumb for Lean rule reference\u0009Rule index\u0009PL4003: Negative examples require completed intended rejection","header":"What a passing result establishes","id":"/rules/PL4003/#PL4003-established"},"/rules/PL5002/#PL5002-established":{"contents":"Every registered public material declaration has a docstring.\n\nIt does not establish:\n\n* That every material declaration is registered, or that the docstring is faithful and adequate (R-DOC); registration completeness is semantic review.* Docstrings of unregistered, private or trivial declarations, which the standard recommends but does not require.\n\nReview obligations the rule-coverage map associates with this rule (identifiers of the checker's Residual account):\n\n* R-DOC: documentation is complete and faithful to the declarations and requirements\n\nEvery accepted result lists all residual obligations as open, whatever rules it checked: R-INTENT, R-INVARIANT, R-LAWS, R-BOUNDARY, R-NONVACUITY, R-DOC, R-COST, R-QUALIFY (and R-GRAPH for a serialized-graph claim). A listed identifier is an open obligation, never a completed review.\n\nEvery accepted result also relies on these trusted mechanisms (the checker's Trusted account), which no rule verifies:\n\n* toolchain: Lean elaborator, kernel and compiler at the snapshot's toolchain identity* acquisition: manifest parsing, Lake loading, and source, configuration and dependency acquisition* extraction: environment extraction, compiler and worker processes, and JSON transport* runtime: native runtime and every execution boundary reported as trusted\n\n","context":"Plumb for Lean rule reference\u0009Rule index\u0009PL5002: Registered public material declarations require docstrings","header":"What a passing result establishes","id":"/rules/PL5002/#PL5002-established"}});